Ss2 marketing 
SECTION A
Instruction: Choose the correct answer from options A–D. Each question carries equal marks

1. A manufacturer chooses road transportation to distribute perishable goods to nearby retailers because it offers ______.
A. the lowest cost for every type of cargo
B. door-to-door delivery and flexibility
C. the highest carrying capacity
D. freedom from traffic congestion

2. Which of the following combinations correctly identifies the major types of transportation?
A. Road, rail, water, air and pipeline
B. Road, banking, rail, insurance and air
C. Rail, warehousing, road, water and advertising
D. Air, road, production, water and pipeline

3. A major economic importance of transportation in marketing is that it ______.
A. eliminates the need for middlemen
B. makes goods available where they are demanded
C. determines the quality of all goods
D. prevents changes in market prices

4. Which of the following measures would most directly address the problem of traffic congestion affecting the distribution of goods?
A. Increasing advertising expenditure
B. Establishing more warehouses without improving roads
C. Improving road infrastructure and traffic management
D. Increasing the price of transported goods

5. A manufacturer stores goods during periods of low demand so that they can be supplied when demand rises. This illustrates the role of warehousing in ______.
A. creating time utility
B. creating form utility
C. determining market prices
D. transferring ownership

6. An importer stores goods in a bonded warehouse while waiting to pay customs duties. The major advantage is that ______.
A. the goods become exempt from all taxes
B. payment of customs duties can be deferred until the goods are released
C. the warehouse owner becomes the importer
D. the goods can be sold without complying with customs regulations

7. A market structure describes the ______.
A. physical arrangement of goods in a warehouse
B. organisation of buyers and sellers and the nature of competition in a market
C. method of transporting goods to consumers
D. activities involved in insuring goods

8. A market in which many sellers offer identical products and no single seller can significantly influence the market price is known as ______.
A. monopoly
B. oligopoly
C. perfect competition
D. monopolistic competition

9. A market association consisting of traders who agree to maintain standards, share market information and settle disputes performs the function of ______.
A. regulating and coordinating members’ activities
B. determining government monetary policy
C. producing all goods sold by members
D. eliminating the need for consumers

10. Which of the following is a likely benefit of a market association to its members?
A. Guaranteed profits regardless of market conditions
B. Protection from every form of competition
C. Collective representation and access to market information
D. Freedom to disregard all market regulations

11. Marketing facilitators are individuals or organisations that assist in the movement of goods and services from producers to consumers without necessarily taking ownership of the goods. Which of the following is an example?
A. Transport company
B. Final consumer
C. Household producer
D. Government legislator

12. A business engages an insurance company to protect its goods against specified risks during transportation. The insurance company is acting as a marketing facilitator by providing ______.
A. product manufacturing
B. risk protection
C. product consumption
D. physical production

13. Which of the following correctly matches a marketing facilitator with its function?
A. Advertising agency — promotes products to target audiences
B. Transport company — manufactures goods
C. Insurance company — determines consumer tastes
D. Warehouse operator — fixes national interest rates

14. The major importance of marketing facilitators is that they ______.
A. eliminate all distribution costs
B. prevent direct contact between producers and consumers
C. make distribution more efficient by providing specialised services
D. guarantee that every product will be sold

15. Which of the following is a characteristic of mineral products that influences their marketing and distribution?
A. They are all produced in urban retail markets
B. Their extraction often requires specialised equipment and substantial capital
C. They can all be transported through pipelines
D. They have no international demand

16. A petroleum company transports crude oil from an oil-producing field to a refinery through a network of pipes. This illustrates the use of ______.
A. road transportation
B. rail transportation
C. pipeline transportation
D. inland water transportation

17. One major problem affecting the marketing of mineral products in Nigeria is ______.
A. the complete absence of international demand
B. inadequate infrastructure and insecurity in some producing areas
C. the inability to use specialised equipment
D. the fact that all mineral products are perishable

18. Which of the following offers the greatest prospect for improving the marketing of Nigeria’s mineral products?
A. Discouraging investment in processing facilities
B. Depending entirely on the export of unprocessed minerals
C. Improving infrastructure and developing local processing industries
D. Restricting the use of modern extraction technology

19. International marketing occurs when a business ______.
A. sells exclusively within its immediate community
B. markets goods or services across national borders
C. buys goods only from local manufacturers
D. distributes products within one state

20. A company planning to sell its products in another country should first investigate that country’s ______.
A. domestic market conditions, consumer preferences and regulations
B. local warehouse colours
C. employees’ personal shopping habits
D. unrelated political activities

21. Which of the following is a major role of government in international marketing?
A. Preventing all exports
B. Eliminating every foreign competitor
C. Establishing trade policies and negotiating trade agreements
D. Fixing the prices of every product sold internationally

22. A Nigerian manufacturer sells products to an independent company in Ghana, which then distributes them to local retailers. This method of entering the international market is known as ______.
A. direct investment
B. exporting through an intermediary
C. franchising
D. establishing a wholly owned subsidiary

23. A Nigerian company allows a foreign business to use its brand name and business model in return for agreed payments. This is an example of ______.
A. franchising
B. direct exporting
C. countertrade
D. indirect taxation

24. Which of the following is a major challenge in international marketing?
A. Differences in language, culture and legal requirements
B. The availability of different markets
C. Access to international customers
D. The possibility of increasing sales

25. Information and Communication Technology (ICT) refers to the use of digital technologies to ______.
A. manufacture mineral resources only
B. collect, process, store and communicate information
C. eliminate all physical markets
D. replace every marketing employee

26. A Nigerian exporter uses video conferencing to negotiate with a foreign buyer without travelling abroad. This demonstrates how ICT ______.
A. increases geographical barriers
B. reduces communication costs and facilitates international business
C. prevents businesses from entering foreign markets
D. eliminates the need for product quality

27. Which of the following best describes e-commerce?
A. The physical movement of goods by road
B. The storage of products in bonded warehouses
C. The buying and selling of goods and services through electronic networks
D. The production of goods using machines

28. A company pays to display promotional messages to selected audiences on social media. This is an example of ______.
A. digital advertising
B. physical distribution
C. warehousing
D. market segmentation only

29. Which of the following is a major advantage of digital marketing platforms to businesses?
A. They restrict businesses to local customers
B. They prevent communication with consumers
C. They guarantee that every advertisement generates sales
D. They enable businesses to reach wider audiences and measure campaign performance

30. A Nigerian clothing business receives orders through its website, accepts electronic payments and uses courier services to deliver products to customers abroad. This business is combining ______.
A. warehousing and traditional barter only
B. e-commerce, electronic payment and international distribution
C. market associations and product manufacturing only
D. insurance and pipeline transportation

